-- title:
--   Germs prime to P become a unit ratio near ξ_P
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be an integral scheme (so, in particular, $X$ is irreducible and reduced, and its function field `X.functionField` is the stalk at the generic point, into which every local ring $\mathcal O_{X,y}$ maps canonically), let $x$ be a point of $X$, and let $P$ be a prime ideal of the local ring $\mathcal O_{X,x} =$ `X.presheaf.stalk x`. Let $a, b \in \mathcal O_{X,x}$ be two germs, neither of which lies in $P$. Write $\xi_P \in X$ for the image of the point of $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_{X,x}$ corresponding to $P$ under the canonical morphism `X.fromSpecStalk x` $\colon \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_{X,x} \to X$; this $\xi_P$ is a generisation of $x$. The assertion is that there exists an open subset $U \subseteq X$ with $\xi_P \in U$ such that for every point $y \in U$ there is a unit $u \in \mathcal O_{X,y}^{\times}$ whose image in the function field of $X$ satisfies $u \cdot b = a$, the images of $a$ and $b$ being taken under the canonical map $\mathcal O_{X,x} \to K(X)$. No claim is made that $x$ itself lies in $U$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that on an integral scheme the ratio of two germs at $x$ that avoid a prime $P \subseteq \mathcal O_{X,x}$ is a unit germ at all points of some open neighbourhood of the associated generisation $\xi_P$; it is proved by clearing denominators on an affine open around $x$ and passing to a basic open set. It is used in the analysis of $q$-expansions on an integral model of a modular curve, where $x$ is a point of a cusp section, $P$ the prime generated by the residue characteristic, and $\xi_P$ the generic point of the special fibre, to produce a non-empty open subset of that fibre on which a prescribed ratio of germs is a regular unit.
